# The app layer ships the tracked files byte for byte, and the release
# workflow asserts that the Windows-built layer is identical to the macOS one.
# Leaving line endings to the checking-out user's core.autocrlf makes that
# assertion depend on ambient configuration: on a machine with autocrlf=true
# every text file arrives as CRLF and the layer differs. The runtime id is
# worse -- it is a SHA-256 over the raw bytes of the two requirements files, so
# a CRLF checkout mints a content-addressed id that no other platform will ever
# produce, and both the "reuse a published runtime" release step and the
# updater's "does the runtime need replacing" decision key on it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are read by cmd.exe, which is not reliably tolerant of LF-only
# line endings around labels and goto. Pin them regardless of the checking-out
# user's core.autocrlf.
*.bat text eol=crlf
